A.1.11. {"problem": "auto_deploy lets the system deploy, but the delivery rules say only a person deploys. Four rules require a person to set `deployed`, bring a request to `deploying`, bring it to `done`, or set `refusal`. Each says every action that does so today requires a person (deploy). auto_deploy has actor `system` and no `requires: human`. It moves built to deploying and calls deploy, whose reply `record_deployment` sets deployed and refusal and moves the request to done. The change contradicts these rules and makes their stated reason ('every action ... requires a person') false.", "where": "delivery.domain.yaml: actions.auto_deploy vs rules only-a-person-deliveryrequest-deployed, -deploying, -done, -refusal", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.1.12. {"problem": "The deploy floor is described as rules the kernel enforces on every deploy that no declaration can weaken, including 'a person deploys'. The `deploy` action says 'Only a person deploys.' An autonomous system deploy conflicts with this floor and will likely be refused at runtime. The glossary, the `deploy` description and the purpose announcement ('lacks a person's approval') were not updated to account for it.", "where": "delivery.domain.yaml: glossary 'deploy floor', actions.deploy description, purpose.announcement", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.1.13. {"problem": "auto_deploy passes `approver: target.approver` and sets `records_decision: true`. That approver is the person who approved the commit in Authoring and handed it off. They did not decide this deploy, so the recorded decision attributes a deployment to someone who did not take it. `deploy` uses `actor.id`, which is the person who actually pressed deploy.", "where": "delivery.domain.yaml: actions.auto_deploy effects.with.approver and records_decision", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
A.1.14. {"problem": "Nothing triggers auto_deploy 'once its preview has passed'. There is no handoff, automation or reaction to `record_build`. After record_build the request stays `built`, and the system action is never invoked. The behaviour the request describes does not happen. There is also no scenario covering auto_deploy: neither the passing path nor the failed-harness refusal.", "where": "delivery.domain.yaml: actions.auto_deploy, record_build, scenarios", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
A.1.15. {"problem": "The diff adds auto_deploy and its matrix cells with `status: proposed`, but the post-change file shows them as `confirmed`. The source is an interview line with no quote, and the change request gives no reason. A permission-affecting change, letting the system deploy where a person did before, appears confirmed without evidence of a person's confirmation. The authoring domain requires two people to confirm elements about who may deploy.", "where": "delivery.domain.yaml: actions.auto_deploy and its transitions cells (status, source)", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.1.16. {"problem": "In the `done` state, auto_deploy is denied with 'Already deployed; deliver again with a new request'. Yet `deploy` from `done` is allowed and goes to `deploying`, which is how a refused deploy such as R3 is retried. The message is wrong for a done request with deployed false, and the two deploy paths are inconsistent. The harness-passed precondition on auto_deploy also has no source or status, unlike its counterpart on `deploy`.", "where": "delivery.domain.yaml: transitions.DeliveryRequest.done.auto_deploy vs done.deploy; actions.auto_deploy.preconditions", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
